c.push(gap());

// ---------------- 3 Theory ----------------
c.push(H1('3. Theoretical framework'));
c.push(...figure('arch', 'fig1_architecture.png', 'Coupled model architecture. Pathways P1 and P2 feed hydration, which sets porosity, strength and stiffness; these drive transport and damage, which determine service life; carbon accounting is evaluated per m\u00B3 and per year of life.', 6.3));
c.push(P('All quantities are in SI-consistent units; Appendix B lists symbols and units. Damage D, stress ratio S and degree of saturation are dimensionless, and every rate in the damage law has units of inverse time. All parameters are uncertain and are sampled (Section 4). This is a **proposed phenomenological framework**, not a validated constitutive model; each component is anchored to an established relation where one exists and its limits are stated.'));
c.push(H2('3.1 Hydration, porosity and strength'));
c.push(P('Hydration degree \u03B1 evolves with a reaction-progress law that can be accelerated or retarded by shell addition:'));
c.push(EQ('d\u03B1/dt = k\u2080 A(T) (1 + \u03BD s(d) r) f_ret (\u03B1_max \u2212 \u03B1)\u207F', '1'));
c.push(P('where k\u2080 is the rate constant at 20 \u00B0C, A(T) = exp[E_a/R (1/293.15 \u2212 1/T)] is the Arrhenius factor, \u03BD is the filler nucleation coefficient, s(d) = min[(30 \u00B5m/d)^0.5, 2] is a fineness factor, f_ret = 1/(1 + \u03BA_o q) is the retardation factor with q = o(1 \u2212 \u03B7_w) r/(1 \u2212 r) the residual organic dose per unit cement (o is the organic fraction and \u03B7_w the washing efficiency), and \u03B1_max = min[\u03B1_cap, (w/c)/0.42] limits the attainable degree to the water available per unit cement. For \u03B1(0) = 0 and n \u2260 1 the closed form is'));
c.push(EQ('\u03B1(t) = \u03B1_max \u2212 [\u03B1_max^(1\u2212n) + (n \u2212 1) k t]^(1/(1\u2212n))', '2'));
c.push(P('Shell addition therefore raises the rate through \u03BD and the ceiling through a larger effective w/c, but lowers the rate through organics, and removes cement. With cement volume v_c, water volume v_w, hydration products of volume 2.06 \u03B1 v_c, the capillary porosity is \u03C6_cap = v_w \u2212 1.06 \u03B1 v_c (Powers and Brownyard 1947) and the gel/space ratio X = 2.06 \u03B1 v_c/(v_w + \u03B1 v_c). Compressive strength is'));
c.push(EQ('f_c = S\u2080\u2032 X^\u03BA [1 + \u03B2_p s r(1 \u2212 r)] (1 \u2212 k_imp \u03C1)', '3'));
c.push(P('where the exponent \u03BA is uncertain (1.8\u20133.0) and S\u2080\u2032 = S\u2080 \u00D7 0.742^(2\u2212\u03BA) keeps the nominal OPC strength at w/c = 0.45 fixed for any \u03BA; \u03B2_p is a packing bonus and k_imp an impurity penalty that applies only to P2. Stiffness, tensile and flexural strength follow E = 4700\u221Af_c, f_t = 0.33\u221Af_c and f_r = 0.62\u221Af_c (MPa; ACI 318).'));
c.push(P('**Effective strength-efficiency coefficient.** In the original model shell powder is an inert diluent in the strength relation. To test this against published data (Section 4.4) an optional coefficient k_f is introduced: the cement volume entering the gel/space ratio is multiplied by [1 + k_f r/(1 \u2212 r)], so that a unit mass of shell counts as k_f units of cement for strength, as in the classical k-value concept for fillers and supplementary materials. k_f is an empirical lump of packing, nucleation, organic retardation, particle-size and other unresolved effects; it is not the chemical binding capacity of shell and a value of 0.65 does not mean that shell has 65% of the binding capacity of cement. Hydration, porosity and transport are not changed in the primary formulation; two alternatives (Section 7.4) couple k_f to the porosity or make it depend on particle size. k_f = 0 recovers the original model.'));
c.push(H2('3.2 Transport and chemical degradation'));
c.push(P('**Chloride.** One-dimensional diffusion with spatially uniform, age-dependent diffusivity has the solution C(x,t) = C_s erfc[x/(2\u221A\u03C4)] with \u03C4 = \u222B D dt (Crank 1975). The diffusivity is'));
c.push(EQ('D(t) = D\u2082\u2088 f_T (28 d / t_age)^m [1 + \u03BA_c D_mech(t)],   m = m\u2080 + \u03C9 r', '4'));
c.push(P('where f_T is an Arrhenius temperature factor (activation energy 35\u201350 kJ/mol; Life-365 uses 44.6 kJ/mol), m\u2080 \u2248 0.2 (Thomas and Bentz 2002), \u03C9 an uncertain filler shift and \u03BA_c couples mechanical damage to transport (cracks raise diffusivity). Because the damage trajectory does not depend on chloride, the coupling is exact for constant surface chloride. The 28-day diffusivity is a blend of two mappings with an uncertain weight \u03C7_D ~ U(0,1), which represents **model-form uncertainty**:'));
c.push(EQ('ln D\u2082\u2088 = (1 \u2212 \u03C7_D) ln D_\u03C6 + \u03C7_D ln D_wc', '5'));
c.push(P(`D_\u03C6 = \u03BB_D D_ref (\u03C6_p/\u03C6_ref)^(\u03B6 + \u0394\u03B6) is a porosity mapping, with the paste capillary porosity \u03C6_p and the exponent \u03B6 = ${f2(cal.zeta)} fitted to the Life-365 relation D\u2082\u2088 = 10^(\u221212.06 + 2.40 w/cm) m\u00B2/s for OPC over w/c = 0.30\u20130.60 (model-to-Life-365 ratio within ${f2(cal.max_ratio)}); D_wc applies the same Life-365 relation to the cement-only water-to-cement ratio, which treats filler as an inert diluent (pessimistic). Applying a mapping calibrated on OPC to filler-bearing pastes is an extrapolation, which is why both are retained. Corrosion initiates when C(x_cover, t) reaches a critical chloride content and a propagation period follows (Tuutti 1982).`));
c.push(P('**Acid and leaching fronts.** For a sharp reaction front, x dx/dt = D_e C_a/N gives x = \u221A(2 \u222B D_e dt C_a/N), where C_a is the aggressive-species concentration and N the capacity of the matrix per unit volume. For acid attack, N = 2[\u03BE_N \u00B7 11.6 \u03B1 c + \u03C7(d) m_calcite/0.10009] (mol H\u207A/m\u00B3), where cement supplies 11.6 mol Ca per kg, \u03BE_N is the accessible share, and calcite supplies 2 mol H\u207A per mole but only when particles are fine enough: \u03C7(d) = 1/[1 + (d/d_c)\u00B2]. For soft-water leaching, N = \u03BE_L \u00B7 11.6 \u03B1 c (calcite does not contribute because of its low solubility), and a pressure-driven term x_a = K i C_sat t/N is added in quadrature to the diffusive front, with hydraulic conductivity K = \u03BA_K D_e. Mass loss from dissolved calcite is not tracked.'));
c.push(H2('3.3 Damage accumulation'));
c.push(P('Total damage is the sum of a mechanical state D_m integrated in time and chemical and abrasion terms that are explicit functions of time:'));
c.push(EQ('dD_m/dt = n_traf / N_f(S_eff) + n_ft d_ft(S_w)', '6'));
c.push(EQ('D(t) = D\u2080 + D_m(t) + D_f x_chem(t)/x_lim + D_f w_abr(t)/w_lim', '7'));
c.push(P('Fatigue life follows the Aas-Jakobsen relation log\u2081\u2080N_f = (1 \u2212 S)/[0.0685(1 \u2212 R)] with stress ratio R = 0.1, and the effective stress ratio S_eff = (\u03C3_traf + \u03C3_env)/[f_r(t)(1 \u2212 D)] includes the continuum-damage amplification 1/(1 \u2212 D), so that damage accelerates as the section degrades. The traffic stress \u03C3_traf is a fixed fraction (low, medium, high stress ratio class) of the OPC reference flexural strength, so that shell and OPC mixes carry the same absolute load; \u03C3_env is the restrained shrinkage/curling stress. Freeze\u2013thaw damage per cycle is d_ft = d\u2081[(S_w \u2212 S_crit)/(1 \u2212 S_crit)]^q for S_w > S_crit, with S_w = (\u03C6_cap + a_f a_air)/(\u03C6_cap + a_air) the degree of saturation (Fagerlund 1977). The chemical term has the form D_f x/x_lim, i.e. the failure damage D_f is reached exactly when the front reaches its limit, which fixes the coefficient B of a generic chemical driver by dimensional consistency rather than leaving it free. Early-age damage D\u2080 is switched on by a logistic function of the cracking indices I_sh = max(\u03C3_sh/f_t) for restrained shrinkage (pavement) or I_th for restrained thermal contraction (dam); a mass-concrete temperature drop is \u0394T = \u03C8_T c Q_cem \u03B1(5 d)/(\u03C1 c_p).'));
c.push(P('Service life is the earliest of: chloride initiation plus propagation, and D reaching D_f; it is censored at 100 years. Because OPC reference concrete often survives the horizon, non-inferiority is defined with a tie-break: when the OPC reference survives 100 years, the shell mix must also survive and its damage index at 100 years must not exceed the reference by more than 5% of D_f.'));
c.push(H2('3.4 Carbon accounting'));
c.push(EQ('E_total = E_binder + E_shell + E_aggregate + E_water/admix + E_construction \u2212 E_credits', '8'));
c.push(P('The functional unit is 1 m\u00B3 of concrete in service; cradle-to-gate embodied carbon E_gate excludes construction, and the annualised value divides E_total by service life. Cement carries a footprint EF_cem (0.75\u20130.95 kg CO\u2082e/kg). For P1, shell powder carries EF_sh = [e_dry EF_th + E_g(d) EF_el]/1000 + (distance \u00D7 EF_truck)/1000 \u2212 c_land per kg, where grinding energy scales as E_g = E_ref (45 \u00B5m/d)^0.5 and c_land is a small landfill-avoidance credit. For P2 the change in cement footprint per kg is \u0394EF = \u03C1 m_CaCO\u2083 [EF_proc \u2212 EF_lq + EF_kiln] \u2212 \u03B2_bio \u03C1 m_CaCO\u2083 \u00D7 0.4397, where 0.4397 kg CO\u2082/kg CaCO\u2083 is the stoichiometric calcination emission and \u03B2_bio is the fraction of that emission credited as biogenic. **The base case sets \u03B2_bio = 0**: shell calcium carbonate does not eliminate the CO\u2082 released on calcination. Organic CO\u2082 is treated as biogenic.'));
c.push(H2('3.5 Falsification region'));
c.push(P('For uncertain parameters \u03B8, let E be embodied carbon and L service life. The shell alternative fails to deliver a simultaneous advantage over its OPC reference, on the same draw of \u03B8, in the set'));
c.push(EQ('\u2131 = { (r, \u03B8) : E_shell \u2265 E_OPC  \u2228  L_shell not non-inferior to L_OPC }', '9'));
c.push(P('The probability P(\u2131) is estimated by Monte Carlo and its complement P_adv is reported. Because carbon differences of a fraction of a percent are within the noise of the inputs, a second, material version requires E_shell \u2264 0.95 E_OPC and is reported as P_adv,mat. Both are given; the falsification maps use the material version.'));

// ---------------- 4 Numerical methodology ----------------
c.push(H1('4. Numerical methodology'));
c.push(H2('4.1 Cases, parameters and sampling'));
c.push(P(`Three cases are modelled with case-specific mix designs, exposures and mechanisms (Appendix A): (A) highway rigid pavement (fatigue, shrinkage, freeze\u2013thaw, chloride from de-icing salt), (B) dam mass concrete (early-age thermal cracking, soft-water leaching with hydrostatic gradient, freeze\u2013thaw), and (C) marine culvert / riverbed (chloride, acid, abrasion). The parameter study varies shell replacement (r = 10, 25, 50, 75, 100%), median particle size (10, 30, 100, 200 \u00B5m), chemical exposure (mild, moderate, severe), mechanical stress ratio (low, medium, high), relative humidity (55\u2013100%), temperature (5\u201360 \u00B0C curing in the hydration study) and horizon (1\u2013100 yr). The P2 pathway is varied over \u03C1 = 25\u2013100% and \u03B2_bio = 0, 50, 100%. For P1, two design rules are compared: **same w/b** (shell replaces cement at constant water and binder content, as in the source white paper) and **strength-matched** (binder content is increased at constant water until the 28-day strength equals the OPC reference; the search is restricted to the range in which the model strength increases with binder, and draws where matching is infeasible are flagged; ${pc(row1('A', 0.25, 30, true).matched)} of draws are matchable at r = 25%).`));
c.push(P(`Uncertain inputs (${Object.keys(PR.common).length} common and up to ${Object.keys(PR.cases.B).length - 1} case-specific parameters) are drawn by Latin hypercube sampling (McKay et al. 1979) with n = ${R.n}. Every shell configuration is evaluated on the same draws as its OPC reference (common random numbers), so differences are attributable to the material and not to sampling noise. Global sensitivity uses Sobol total-order indices with the Jansen estimator (Saltelli et al. 2010; Sobol 2001), n = ${R.sobol_n || 2048} base samples with bootstrap intervals. The cases are time-stepped at 0.125 yr; hydration, chloride and front solutions are closed form or cumulative integrals.`));
c.push(H2('4.2 Baseline anchoring and calibration'));
c.push(P(`Three calibration steps use only the OPC reference. (i) The porosity\u2013diffusivity exponent is fitted to the Life-365 relation (\u03B6 = ${f2(cal.zeta)}; maximum deviation factor ${f2(cal.max_ratio)}). (ii) The strength model is set so that nominal OPC strength at w/c = 0.45 is about 43 MPa and the strength\u2013w/c slope resembles an Abrams-type curve. (iii) Traffic cycles, curling stress, stress-ratio classes, surface chloride and cover are chosen so that the OPC reference reaches plausible design lives (median ${f1(opc('A').L[1])} yr for the pavement, ${f1(opc('C').L[1])} yr for the culvert, 98% survival to 100 yr for the dam). The last step anchors the absolute scale of the service life, so results should be read as **relative to the reference**, not as absolute life predictions. Shell-specific parameters are not calibrated in these three steps; they are tested and calibrated against published data in Section 4.4.`));
c.push(H2('4.3 Verification and validation'));
c.push(TC('ver', 'Numerical verification (python ShellConcreteMC_v2.py --verify).'));
c.push(table(['Test', 'Result'], [
  ['Baseline recovery: r = 0 (P1) and \u03C1 = 0 (P2) reproduce OPC life and carbon', `max difference ${Math.max(...Object.values(ver.baseline_recovery_max_abs_diff))}`],
  ['Closed-form hydration vs fourth-order Runge\u2013Kutta integration', `max error ${ver.hydration_closed_form_vs_rk4_max_abs_err.toExponential(1)}`],
  ['Time-varying-D chloride solution vs Crank\u2013Nicolson finite differences', `max error ${ver.chloride_erfc_vs_finite_difference.max_rel_err_vs_Cs.toExponential(1)} of C_s`],
  ['Damage time-step convergence (\u0394t = 0.125 vs 0.0625 yr)', `median ${pc(ver.damage_step_convergence.median_rel_diff, 1)}; 95th percentile ${pc(ver.damage_step_convergence.p95_rel_diff, 1)} difference in life`],
  ['Carbon budget closure (sum of parts = total)', `residual ${ver.carbon_closure_max_abs}`],
  ['Calcination stoichiometry', `${ver.calcination_stoichiometry_kgCO2_per_kgCaCO3.toFixed(4)} kg CO\u2082/kg CaCO\u2083`],
  ['Strength matching reproduces the OPC 28-day strength (matched draws)', `max relative error ${ver.strength_matching_max_rel_err_matched_draws.toExponential(1)}; ${pc(ver.strength_matching_feasible_fraction_r25)} feasible at r = 25%`],
  ['D\u2082\u2088 vs Life-365 relation, OPC, w/c = 0.30\u20130.60', `within a factor ${f2(cal.max_ratio)}`],
], [6000, 3360]));
c.push(gap());
c.push(...figure('ver', 'fig9_verification.png', 'Baseline checks. (a) Model D\u2082\u2088 against the Life-365 relation for OPC. (b) Model OPC 28-day strength against w/c (P10\u2013P90). (c) Median relative 28-day strength against shell replacement (P1, 25 \u00B5m).', 6.3));
c.push(P('**Verification is not validation.** The checks above show that the equations are solved correctly and that the baseline is recovered; they say nothing about whether the model describes shell concrete. The model is compared with published directional behaviour (' + T('qual') + ') and, in Section 4.4, with the only open quantitative dataset found. Conformity with directional trends is a necessary, not sufficient, condition.'));
c.push(TC('qual', 'Directional checks of the model against published trends for ground shell as cement replacement.'));
c.push(table(['Published trend', 'Model check', 'Result'], [
  ['Strength decreases monotonically with replacement (5\u201350%)', 'P(strength decreases at every step), r = 0\u201350%', pc(qc.strength_monotone_decrease_prob)],
  ['Strength relative to OPC at 10 / 20 / 50% replacement', 'Median relative 28-day strength', `${f2(qc.relative_strength_28d_median['10%'])} / ${f2(qc.relative_strength_28d_median['20%'])} / ${f2(qc.relative_strength_28d_median['50%'])}`],
  ['Porosity / water absorption increases with replacement', 'P(capillary porosity at 50% > at 0%)', pc(qc.capillary_porosity_increase_prob_0_to_50)],
  ['Drying shrinkage decreases (some shell types)', 'P(shrinkage proxy at 20% < at 0%)', pc(qc.shrinkage_proxy_decrease_prob_0_to_20)],
  ['Setting / early hydration retarded by shell organics', 'P(\u03B1(1 d) lower; unwashed shell, no filler effect)', pc(qc.early_age_retardation_prob_unwashed_no_filler_effect)],
], [3800, 3500, 2060]));
c.push(gap());


c.push(H2('4.4 Out-of-sample tests, calibration and the strength-efficiency coefficient'));
c.push(H2('4.4.1 Calibration on a scallop-shell dataset'));
const KF0 = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('shell_out/kf0_pred.json')).A;
const MXN = ['S0F15', 'S15F15', 'S30F15'];
const rng = (a) => `${f2(a[1])} (${f2(a[0])}\u2013${f2(a[2])})`;
c.push(P(`Validation needs measured data. The first dataset used is Abbas and Kudukkan (2025), who used scallop shell as filler and partial cement replacement at a binder content of 350 kg/m\u00B3 and a water content of 160 kg/m\u00B3. The three mixes contain ${Math.round(CAL.mixes.S0F15.r * 350)}, ${Math.round(CAL.mixes.S15F15.r * 350)} and ${Math.round(CAL.mixes.S30F15.r * 350)} kg/m\u00B3 of shell-derived powder (r = ${pc(CAL.mixes.S0F15.r)}, ${pc(CAL.mixes.S15F15.r)} and ${pc(CAL.mixes.S30F15.r)} of the binder) with the median particle sizes reported there. Observed 28-day strengths relative to the control are read from the text for two mixes (\u201Calmost the same\u201D, \u201Cnearly 25% reduction\u201D) and derived for the third from the reported moduli of elasticity (E \u221D \u221Af_c); they are approximate, and a tolerance of \u00B1${CAL.tol} on the relative strength represents this and specimen scatter.`));
c.push(TC('abbas', 'Calibration set: strength model against Abbas and Kudukkan (2025), 28-day compressive strength relative to the control mix, median (P10\u2013P90) of model draws. Observed values: S0F15 and S30F15 from the text of the source, S15F15 derived from the reported moduli. The strength model is shared by all three cases.'));
c.push(table(['Mix', 'Shell powder, r', 'Median size (\u00B5m)', 'Observed', 'Original model (k_f = 0)', 'With k_f prior', 'Calibrated (posterior)', 'Role'],
  MXN.map((k) => [k, pc(CAL.mixes[k].r), String(CAL.mixes[k].d_um), f2(CAL.obs[k]), rng(KF0.pred[k]), rng(CA.prior_pred[k]), rng(CA.post_pred[k]), k === 'S15F15' ? 'held out' : 'calibration']),
  [900, 900, 900, 900, 1560, 1560, 1560, 1080], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`The original model predicts relative strengths of ${f2(KF0.pred.S0F15[1])}, ${f2(KF0.pred.S15F15[1])} and ${f2(KF0.pred.S30F15[1])} against ${f2(CAL.obs.S0F15)}, ${f2(CAL.obs.S15F15)} and ${f2(CAL.obs.S30F15)} observed; none of 100,000 prior draws falls within the tolerance for any mix, so the inert-filler strength model is rejected by these data. Adding k_f (prior U(0, 1.2)) and running rejection approximate Bayesian computation on S0F15 and S30F15 (300,000 draws, tolerance ${CAL.tol}) accepted ${f0(CA.n_accepted)} draws (${pc(CA.accept_rate, 1)}); S15F15 was held out. The posterior median of k_f is ${f2(kfp.post_median)} (P10\u2013P90 ${f2(kfp.post_p10)}\u2013${f2(kfp.post_p90)}), and the posterior predictive for the held-out mix is ${f2(CA.post_pred.S15F15[0])}\u2013${f2(CA.post_pred.S15F15[2])} against ${f2(CAL.obs.S15F15)} observed (${F('abc')}a). This is an interpolation between the calibration mixes, so it is a weak test, and a bias of 0.06\u20130.07 remains at the calibration mixes (${f2(CA.post_pred.S0F15[1])} against ${f2(CAL.obs.S0F15)} and ${f2(CA.post_pred.S30F15[1])} against ${f2(CAL.obs.S30F15)}), indicating a residual structural misfit of the strength relation. The carbon accounting was also checked against the same paper: with its emission factors (cement 0.91, shell powder 0.059 kg CO\u2082e/kg) the model arithmetic gives ${sg(CAL.carbon_check.S15F15.published_reduction_pct)}% and ${sg(CAL.carbon_check.S30F15.published_reduction_pct)}%, the same budget the model uses (a consistency check, not an independent test), and the model\u2019s own shell emission factor at 55 \u00B5m (${CAL.shell_ef_model.p50.toFixed(3)} kg CO\u2082e/kg, P10\u2013P90 ${CAL.shell_ef_model.p10.toFixed(3)}\u2013${CAL.shell_ef_model.p90.toFixed(3)}) is of the same order as the laboratory value of 0.059 whose boundary is not stated.`));
c.push(...figure('abc', 'fig11_calibration.png', 'Calibration against Abbas and Kudukkan (2025). (a) Relative 28-day strength: prior predictive with the k_f prior (grey), calibrated posterior predictive (blue) and observed values with the \u00B1' + CAL.tol + ' tolerance (red); S15F15 was not used in the calibration. (b) Posterior 10th\u201390th percentile range of selected parameters relative to their prior medians (circle: posterior median).', 6.4));
c.push(H2('4.4.2 Independent oyster-shell data with frozen parameters'));
c.push(P(`The Abbas-calibrated posterior was then frozen and used to predict three independent sets of oyster-shell data without refitting. (i) Kwon and Wang (2024): oyster-shell powder replacing 15% and 30% of cement in mortar at constant binder (568 kg/m\u00B3) and water-to-binder ratio 0.50 (Tables 1 and 3 of the source; observed relative strengths ${f2(VAL.datasets.KWON.items[0].observed)} and ${f2(VAL.datasets.KWON.items[1].observed)}; particle size not reported, 30 \u00B5m assumed). (ii) Abinaya et al. as transcribed in Table A1 of Kim and Lee (2023): oyster shell at 2.5\u201310% replacement, water-to-binder ratio 0.40, observed relative strengths ${VAL.datasets.ABIN.items.map((i) => f2(i.observed)).join(', ')}; this is a secondary source, binder content (400 kg/m\u00B3) and particle size (30 \u00B5m) are assumed. (iii) De Dios-Su\u00E1rez et al. (2026): untreated oyster-shell CaCO\u2083 at 10 wt% of cement in 1:4 mortar (water-to-cement ratio 0.68) for five sieve fractions of 0.14\u20130.84 mm (observed relative strengths ${VAL.datasets.MEX.items.map((i) => f2(i.observed)).join(', ')}); the 10 wt% is interpreted as replacement and the sieve apertures as particle sizes, both uncertain. Eleven data points result. The calibrated model is compared with the inert model, a k-value rule with k_f = 0.25 in the same model, and the Bolomey dilution relation f \u221D (c/w \u2212 0.5) without and with a k-value of 0.25; the same errors are reported for all.`));
c.push(TC('valid', 'Prediction of independent oyster-shell strength data (11 points, relative to each study\u2019s control) with frozen parameters: mean absolute error (MAE), root-mean-square error (RMSE), bias (prediction minus observation) and, for the draw-based models, the share of observations inside the 5\u201395% predictive interval widened by the 0.07 tolerance.'));
const vrow = (lab, key) => { const g = CMP[lab]; const cv = g.all.coverage90 === undefined ? '\u2013' : pc(g.all.coverage90); return [key, f3m(g.all.MAE), f3m(g.all.RMSE), sg(g.all.bias, 3), cv, f3m(g.KWON.MAE), f3m(g.ABIN.MAE), f3m(g.MEX.MAE)]; };
function f3m(v) { return v.toFixed(3); }
c.push(table(['Model', 'MAE (all)', 'RMSE (all)', 'Bias (all)', '90% interval coverage', 'MAE Kwon & Wang', 'MAE Abinaya', 'MAE mortar 2026'],
  [vrow(mname.abbas, 'Abbas-calibrated, frozen'), vrow(mname.inert, 'Inert filler (k_f = 0)'), vrow(mname.k25, 'k_f = 0.25'), vrow(mname.bol, 'Bolomey dilution'), vrow(mname.bolk, 'Bolomey k-value 0.25')],
  [2300, 900, 900, 900, 1300, 1060, 1000, 1000], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`The calibrated model has the lowest error but not by a meaningful margin (MAE ${f3m(CMP[mname.abbas].all.MAE)} against ${f3m(CMP[mname.inert].all.MAE)} for the inert model and ${f3m(CMP[mname.bol].all.MAE)} for dilution alone), and its errors are not random: it over-predicts the large strength loss of Kwon and Wang by ${f2(CMP[mname.abbas].KWON.bias)} (predicted ${f2(VAL.frozen.rows[0].post[1])} and ${f2(VAL.frozen.rows[1].post[1])} against ${f2(VAL.datasets.KWON.items[0].observed)} and ${f2(VAL.datasets.KWON.items[1].observed)}) and under-predicts the small strength gains of the other two studies. The only open oyster-shell binary dataset with a large replacement therefore loses more strength than pure dilution would, which no filler-efficiency in the range 0\u20131 can reproduce. The calibrated model does not outperform simple rules on independent data, and this is reported as a result: the calibration of one scallop-shell study is not evidence that the strength of oyster-shell concrete is predicted.`));
c.push(H2('4.4.3 Study-level filler efficiency and the pooled predictive distribution'));
c.push(P(`To describe what the four datasets say about a new study, k_f was estimated separately for each (rejection ABC on the study\u2019s mixes, k_f ~ U(\u22121.5, 1.5), tolerance 0.07; ${T('kfstudy')}). The estimates differ in sign and size: ${f2(KFE.KWON)} for Kwon and Wang (strength loss larger than dilution), ${f2(KFE.ABBAS)} for Abbas and Kudukkan, ${f2(KFE.MEX)} for the 2026 mortar study and ${f2(KFE.ABIN)} for Abinaya et al. (strength gains at low replacement). Treating the study estimates as a random sample, k_f for a new study has a Student-t predictive distribution (reference prior) with location ${f2(KRE.mu)}, scale ${f2(KRE.scale)} and ${KRE.df} degrees of freedom, truncated to [\u22121, 1.5] so that the effective cementing mass is not negative. This distribution, which expresses between-study heterogeneity and not a property of oyster shell, replaces the single posterior in all primary results below. In leave-one-study-out tests it predicts the held-out study with a mean absolute error of ${f3m(LOSO.ABBAS.MAE)}, ${f3m(LOSO.KWON.MAE)}, ${f3m(LOSO.ABIN.MAE)} and ${f3m(LOSO.MEX.MAE)} for Abbas, Kwon, Abinaya and the 2026 mortar study, and its 90% interval covers every held-out point only because it is very wide.`));
c.push(TC('kfstudy', 'Study-level effective strength-efficiency coefficient k_f (rejection ABC, median and P10\u2013P90) and leave-one-study-out prediction by the pooled distribution.'));
const kfrow = (key, lab, ev) => { const e = VAL.per_study_kf[key]; return [lab, e.median !== undefined ? `${f2(e.median)} (${f2(e.p10)}\u2013${f2(e.p90)})` : `best fit ${f2(e.best_grid_kf)}`, String(e.n_accepted), `${f3m(LOSO[key].MAE)} / ${sg(LOSO[key].bias, 3)}`, ev]; };
c.push(table(['Study', 'k_f', 'Accepted draws', 'Leave-one-out MAE / bias', 'Evidence quality'], [
  kfrow('ABBAS', 'Abbas and Kudukkan 2025 (scallop, concrete)', 'text values and derived moduli; three mixes'),
  kfrow('KWON', 'Kwon and Wang 2024 (oyster, mortar)', 'tabulated values; two mixes; size unknown'),
  kfrow('ABIN', 'Abinaya et al. via Kim and Lee 2023 (oyster)', 'secondary transcription; binder content assumed'),
  kfrow('MEX', 'De Dios-Su\u00E1rez et al. 2026 (oyster CaCO\u2083, mortar)', 'tabulated; coarse fractions; addition vs replacement unclear')], [2800, 1700, 900, 1700, 2260], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(...figure('val', 'fig12_validation.png', 'Independent validation and study-level k_f. (a) Predicted against observed relative strength for 11 independent oyster-shell data points: Abbas-calibrated model with frozen parameters (coloured, median and 5\u201395% interval) and the original inert model (grey crosses). (b) k_f estimated separately for each study and the pooled random-effects predictive interval used for the primary results.', 6.5));
c.push(P('The calibration constrains strength only. Porosity, and hence chloride diffusivity, count cement only in the primary formulation and k_f is not applied to transport; no open dataset with tabulated chloride, acid or freeze\u2013thaw results for shell-powder concrete was found, so the transport and damage predictions remain uncalibrated and are bracketed in Section 7.4. The study-level estimates condition on the other parameters being at their priors, ignore the weak posterior correlations among them, and the two weakest datasets (Abinaya, 2026 mortar) carry assumptions stated above; leaving them out leaves Abbas and Kwon with estimates of opposite sign and does not narrow the pooled interval.'));
// ---------------- 5 Results ----------------
c.push(H1('5. Results'));
c.push(P('Sections 5\u20137 report the primary model, in which the strength-efficiency coefficient k_f follows the pooled random-effects predictive distribution of Section 4.4.3 (all other inputs unchanged). The original inert-filler model and the Abbas-only calibration are reported as ablations in Section 7.5.'));
c.push(H2('5.1 Hydration and microstructure'));
c.push(...figure('hyd', 'fig2_hydration.png', 'Hydration degree against curing time (median and P10\u2013P90): (a) shell replacement fraction; (b) curing temperature; (c) median particle size (P1, w/b = 0.42).', 6.4));
c.push(P(`The model does not predict a large acceleration. Median hydration degree at 1 day is ${f2(alpha.opc[0])} for OPC and ${f2(alpha.r10[0])}, ${f2(alpha.r25[0])} and ${f2(alpha.r50[0])} for 10%, 25% and 50% replacement; at 28 days the values are ${f2(alpha.opc[2])}, ${f2(alpha.r10[2])}, ${f2(alpha.r25[2])} and ${f2(alpha.r50[2])}. The nucleation effect and the larger effective w/c are largely offset by organic retardation and dilution, so the probability that 1-day hydration is higher than OPC at r = 20% is ${pc(qc.early_age_acceleration_prob_default)} (the sign depends on the poorly known filler and retardation coefficients). Meanwhile cement is removed: median 28-day strength falls to ${pc(claims.r25.fc_rel)} of OPC at r = 25% and ${pc(claims.r50.fc_rel)} at r = 50%, and capillary porosity per unit paste rises by ${pc(X.phicap_r25[1] - 1)} and ${pc(X.phicap_r50[1] - 1)}. Temperature (${F('hyd')}b) accelerates hydration through the Arrhenius factor, and finer shell (${F('hyd')}c) accelerates it moderately.`));
c.push(H2('5.2 Transport'));
c.push(...figure('trans', 'fig3_transport.png', 'Transport indicators against shell replacement for same-w/b and strength-matched mixes (median and P10\u2013P90): (a) 28-day chloride diffusivity, pavement; (b) acid front depth at 50 years, severe exposure, culvert; (c) leaching front depth at 50 years, dam.', 6.4));
c.push(P(`At constant w/b the median 28-day chloride diffusivity rises by a factor ${f1(X.D28ratio_r10[1])} at r = 10%, ${f1(X.D28ratio_r25[1])} at 25% and ${f1(X.D28ratio_r50[1])} at 50%, because dilution raises porosity and the cement-only mapping amplifies the effect. In the severe acid case the 50-year front is ${f1(X.front_C.opc[1])} mm for OPC and ${f1(X.front_C.r25u[1])} mm at r = 25% (same w/b, ${sg(100 * (X.front_C.r25u[1] / X.front_C.opc[1] - 1), 0)}%) but ${f1(X.front_C.r25m[1])} mm when strength-matched (${sg(100 * (X.front_C.r25m[1] / X.front_C.opc[1] - 1), 0)}%): calcite buffering helps only when it is not outweighed by dilution. Particle size matters: at r = 25% the front is ${f1(X.front_C.r25_d10[1])} mm for 10 \u00B5m shell and ${f1(X.front_C.r25_d200[1])} mm for 200 \u00B5m shell, because coarse particles are less accessible. For soft-water leaching the 50-year front is ${f1(X.front_B.opc[1])} mm for OPC, ${f1(X.front_B.r25u[1])} mm at r = 25% (${sg(100 * (X.front_B.r25u[1] / X.front_B.opc[1] - 1), 0)}%) and ${f1(X.front_B.r50u[1])} mm at r = 50%, because calcite adds no leachable calcium and dilutes the cement. The calcite-dissolution mass loss in acid, which could offset the buffering benefit, is not modelled.`));
c.push(H2('5.3 Service-life distributions'));
c.push(...figure('life', 'fig4_life.png', 'Empirical cumulative distribution of service life (censored at 100 years) for OPC and selected shell mixes in the three cases (medium load, moderate exposure; 30 \u00B5m shell).', 6.4));
c.push(P(`The OPC reference has a median life of ${f1(opc('A').L[1])} yr (P10\u2013P90 ${f1(opc('A').L[0])}\u2013${f1(opc('A').L[2])}) in the pavement case, ${f1(opc('C').L[1])} yr (${f1(opc('C').L[0])}\u2013${f1(opc('C').L[2])}) in the culvert case, and survives 100 years in ${pc(opc('B').modes['0'])} of dam draws. At constant w/b, shell replacement shifts the distributions to shorter life in the pavement and culvert cases, and strongly so at r \u2265 50% (${F('life')}). Strength-matched mixes recover part of the lost life (median ${sg(A25m.dL[1])} yr at r = 25%, pavement) but with a wide spread, because the filler efficiency differs between studies (Section 4.4.3) and the diffusivity mapping is untested (Section 7.3). Details by case are in Section 6.`));
c.push(H2('5.4 Carbon'));
c.push(...figure('carbon', 'fig10_carbon.png', 'Left: mean embodied carbon by component (pavement mix). Right: change in embodied carbon against shell replacement at constant w/b (median and P10\u2013P90); the dashed line marks the 80% reduction claimed in the source white paper.', 6.4));
c.push(TC('carbon', 'Change in cradle-to-gate embodied carbon relative to OPC (median, P10\u2013P90) and relative 28-day strength; pavement mix, 30 \u00B5m shell.'));
const crow = (lab, e, fc) => [lab, `${sg(e[1])}  (${sg(e[0])} to ${sg(e[2])})`, fc];
c.push(table(['Configuration', 'Carbon change vs OPC (%)', '28-day strength vs OPC'], [
  crow('P1, r = 10%, same w/b', A10.dE_pct, f2(A10.fc28[1] / A10.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P1, r = 25%, same w/b', A25.dE_pct, f2(A25.fc28[1] / A25.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P1, r = 50%, same w/b', A50.dE_pct, f2(A50.fc28[1] / A50.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P1, r = 100%, same w/b', row1('A', 1.0, 30, false).dE_pct, f2(row1('A', 1.0, 30, false).fc28[1] / A25.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P1, r = 25%, strength-matched', A25m.dE_pct, f2(A25m.fc28[1] / A25m.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P1, r = 50%, strength-matched', row1('A', 0.5, 30, true).dE_pct, f2(row1('A', 0.5, 30, true).fc28[1] / A25.fc28_opc[1])),
  crow('P2, \u03C1 = 100%, no biogenic credit', row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct, 'n/a (\u2248 1.0)'),
  crow('P2, \u03C1 = 100%, 50% biogenic credit', row2('A', 1.0, 0.5).dE_pct, 'n/a (\u2248 1.0)'),
  crow('P2, \u03C1 = 100%, 100% biogenic credit', row2('A', 1.0, 1.0).dE_pct, 'n/a (\u2248 1.0)'),
], [4000, 3100, 2260], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`At constant w/b, carbon falls almost proportionally to r (${sg(A10.dE_pct[1])}%, ${sg(A25.dE_pct[1])}%, ${sg(A50.dE_pct[1])}% at 10, 25 and 50%) because the shell powder footprint is small relative to cement. The claimed 80% reduction is reached only at essentially complete cement replacement (${sg(claims.r100.dE_p50)}% at r = 100%), at which the concrete has no strength. When strength is matched by adding binder, the median benefit shrinks to ${sg(claims.r25.dE_matched_p50)}% at r = 25% and ${sg(claims.r50.dE_matched_p50)}% at r = 50%, with a wide interval (P10\u2013P90 ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[0])} to ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[2])}% at 25%) that includes net increases where the effective efficiency is low or negative. Pathway P2 increases embodied carbon by ${f1(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct[1])}% (P10\u2013P90 ${f1(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct[0])} to ${f1(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct[2])}) when no biogenic credit is allowed, because processing and transport of shell exceed the footprint of quarrying adjacent limestone while the calcination CO\u2082 is unchanged; a reduction appears only when 50% or 100% of the calcination CO\u2082 is credited (${f1(row2('A', 1.0, 0.5).dE_pct[1])}% and ${f1(row2('A', 1.0, 1.0).dE_pct[1])}%), which is an accounting choice and not a material property.`));
c.push(H2('5.5 Carbon\u2013durability trade-off'));
c.push(...figure('pareto', 'fig5_pareto.png', 'Embodied carbon per year of life against median service life for OPC, P1 (r \u2264 50%, 30 \u00B5m; same w/b and strength-matched) and P2 without credit. Filled symbols are Pareto-optimal, open symbols are dominated.', 6.4));
c.push(P(`Over the plausible replacement range (r \u2264 50%), no shell mix dominates OPC in every case. Strength-matched P1 mixes cut carbon by a median ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1], 0)}% at r = 25% but lose a median ${f1(-A25m.dL[1])} yr (pavement) and ${f1(-C25m.dL[1])} yr (culvert) of life, whereas same-w/b mixes cut carbon by ${sg(A25.dE_pct[1], 0)}% and lose ${f1(-A25.dL[1])} and ${f1(-C25.dL[1])} yr; in the dam case life is bounded by the 100-year horizon (${F('pareto')}). These are median-based statements; the joint-advantage probabilities in Section 7 give the more informative picture.`));

// ---------------- 6 Infrastructure applications ----------------
c.push(H1('6. Infrastructure applications'));
const caseTable = (key, cs, title) => {
  const o = opc(cs);
  const rows = [['OPC reference', f0(o.fc28), `${f1(o.L[0])} / ${f1(o.L[1])} / ${f1(o.L[2])}`, '\u2013', '\u2013', '\u2013', '\u2013']];
  const add = (lab, x) => rows.push([lab, f0(x.fc28[1]), `${f1(x.L[0])} / ${f1(x.L[1])} / ${f1(x.L[2])}`, sg(x.dL[1]), pc(x.p_L), sg(x.dE_pct[1]), pc(x.p_adv_mat)]);
  [0.10, 0.25, 0.50].forEach((r) => add(`P1 ${Math.round(r * 100)}%, same w/b`, row1(cs, r, 30, false)));
  [0.10, 0.25, 0.50].forEach((r) => add(`P1 ${Math.round(r * 100)}%, matched`, row1(cs, r, 30, true)));
  add('P2 100%, no credit', row2(cs, 1.0, 0.0)); add('P2 100%, 50% credit', row2(cs, 1.0, 0.5));
  return [TC(key, title), table(['Mix', 'f_c28 (MPa)', 'Life P10 / P50 / P90 (yr)', '\u0394L P50 (yr)', 'P(non-inferior)', '\u0394E P50 (%)', 'P(\u22655% cut & non-inf.)'], rows, [1900, 800, 2000, 900, 1100, 1000, 1660], { boldFirst: true }), gap()];
};
c.push(H2('6.1 Case A: highway rigid pavement'));
c.push(P(`The pavement case combines repeated traffic loading, restrained shrinkage and curling, freeze\u2013thaw and chloride ingress from de-icing salt (medium stress ratio, moderate exposure). The OPC failure mode is chloride corrosion initiation plus propagation in ${pc(opc('A').modes['1'])} of draws and damage in ${pc(opc('A').modes['2'])} (${T('pave')}).`));
caseTable('pave', 'A', 'Case A results (30 \u00B5m shell; 3,000 paired draws; medium stress ratio, moderate chloride exposure).').forEach((e) => c.push(e));
c.push(P(`At constant w/b, replacement reduces median life by ${f1(-A10.dL[1])} yr (10%), ${f1(-A25.dL[1])} yr (25%) and ${f1(-A50.dL[1])} yr (50%), since the weaker, more porous paste fatigues and admits chloride faster. The restrained-shrinkage cracking index exceeds 1 in ${pc(A25.p_crack_sh_opc)} of OPC draws and in ${pc(A10.p_crack_sh)}, ${pc(A25.p_crack_sh)} and ${pc(A50.p_crack_sh)} of draws at 10%, 25% and 50% replacement (same w/b), a shrinkage benefit that arises from dilution; with strength matched it is ${pc(A25m.p_crack_sh)}. Strength-matched mixes change median life by ${sg(A25m.dL[1])} yr at r = 25% (P10\u2013P90 ${sg(A25m.dL[0])} to ${sg(A25m.dL[2])}) and carbon by ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1])}%. Sensitivity of the life difference is led by ${top('A', 'dL')}.`));
c.push(H2('6.2 Case B: dam (mass concrete)'));
c.push(P(`For mass concrete the modelled mechanisms (thermal cracking, leaching with hydrostatic gradient, freeze\u2013thaw on the face) rarely end the life of the OPC reference within 100 years (${pc(opc('B').modes['0'])} survive), so differences in life are censored and the damage-index tie-break governs (${T('dam')}). Lower cement content reduces the adiabatic temperature rise, but the OPC thermal cracking probability is already ${pc(B10.p_crack_th_opc)}, so little is gained. At r = 10% (same w/b) the probability of non-inferior life is ${pc(B10.p_L)} and that of a joint 5% carbon cut and non-inferior life is ${pc(B10.p_adv_mat)}; at 25% it falls to ${pc(B25.p_adv_mat)} because the leaching front deepens (Section 5.2), and at 50% it is ${pc(B50.p_adv_mat)}. The apparent success at low replacement is therefore a statement that dam concrete is durable in the model regardless, not evidence of benefit; with strength matched at r = 25% it is ${pc(row1('B', 0.25, 30, true).p_adv_mat)} (${sg(row1('B', 0.25, 30, true).dE_pct[1])}% carbon). The reference here is OPC although dam concrete usually contains supplementary cementitious materials. Life differences are driven by ${top('B', 'dL')}.`));
caseTable('dam', 'B', 'Case B results (30 \u00B5m shell; 3,000 paired draws; moderate exposure). Median life is censored at 100 years for the OPC reference.').forEach((e) => c.push(e));
c.push(H2('6.3 Case C: marine culvert / riverbed'));
c.push(P(`The culvert case combines chloride ingress, acid from organic matter and abrasion. OPC failure is by chloride corrosion in ${pc(opc('C').modes['1'])} of draws (${T('culv')}). At constant w/b the median life falls by ${f1(-C10.dL[1])} yr at 10%, ${f1(-C25.dL[1])} yr at 25% and ${f1(-C50.dL[1])} yr at 50%, and the probability of non-inferior life is ${pc(C10.p_L)}, ${pc(C25.p_L)} and ${pc(C50.p_L)}: shell concrete underperforms OPC in essentially every draw when strength is not matched. Strength matching reduces the median loss (${sg(C25m.dL[1])} yr at 25%) and gives non-inferior life in ${pc(C25m.p_L)} of draws, but the joint-advantage probability is at most ${pc(C25m.p_adv_mat)} because the draws with non-inferior life are mostly those in which the matched mix needs so much added cement that the carbon cut is below 5%. The acid benefit of calcite buffering (Section 5.2) is too small relative to chloride-driven life to alter the failure mode. Life differences are driven by ${top('C', 'dL')}.`));
caseTable('culv', 'C', 'Case C results (30 \u00B5m shell; 3,000 paired draws; moderate exposure).').forEach((e) => c.push(e));
c.push(H2('6.4 Comparison with a low-clinker blended-cement reference'));
c.push(P(`Plain OPC is not what engineers would choose today for marine or mass concrete. A blended-cement reference was therefore added: 50% of the binder replaced by ground granulated blast-furnace slag, with the Life-365 treatment of slag (age exponent of the diffusivity raised by 0.4 \u00D7 0.5/0.7, 28-day diffusivity at equal water-to-binder ratio unchanged), a slag emission factor of 0.07 kg CO\u2082e/kg (0.12 as a sensitivity), identical 28-day strength and the same acid and leaching treatment as OPC; this is a screening treatment and not a validated slag-concrete model. The blended reference cuts embodied carbon by ${f0(-BLc('A', 'blend50').dE_pct_vs_opc[1])}% (P10\u2013P90 ${f0(-BLc('A', 'blend50').dE_pct_vs_opc[2])}\u2013${f0(-BLc('A', 'blend50').dE_pct_vs_opc[0])}%) and raises the median life from ${f1(BLc('A', 'opc_L')[1])} to ${f1(BLc('A', 'blend50').L[1])} yr in the pavement case and from ${f1(BLc('C', 'opc_L')[1])} to ${f1(BLc('C', 'blend50').L[1])} yr in the culvert case (${T('blend')}).`));
c.push(TC('blend', 'Shell concrete (P1, r = 25%, 30 \u00B5m, primary model) against plain OPC and against the 50% slag blended-cement reference: median change in embodied carbon and in service life (P10\u2013P90), and P(\u22655% carbon cut and non-inferior life).'));
const brow = (lab, c, k) => { const x = BLc(c, k); return [lab, `${sg(x.dE_pct[1], 0)} (${sg(x.dE_pct[0], 0)} to ${sg(x.dE_pct[2], 0)})`, `${sg(x.dL[1])} (${sg(x.dL[0])} to ${sg(x.dL[2])})`, f2(x.p_adv_mat)]; };
const brows = [];
for (const [c2, nm] of [['A', 'Pavement'], ['B', 'Dam'], ['C', 'Culvert']]) {
  brows.push([`${nm}: blended reference vs OPC`, `${sg(BLc(c2, 'blend50').dE_pct_vs_opc[1], 0)}`, `${sg(BLc(c2, 'blend50').dL_vs_opc[1])}`, '\u2013']);
  brows.push(brow(`${nm}: shell 25%, same w/b vs OPC`, c2, 'P1_r25u_vs_opc')); brows.push(brow(`${nm}: shell 25%, matched vs OPC`, c2, 'P1_r25m_vs_opc'));
  brows.push(brow(`${nm}: shell 25%, matched vs blended`, c2, 'P1_r25m_vs_blend50')); brows.push(brow(`${nm}: shell 50%, matched vs blended`, c2, 'P1_r50m_vs_blend50'));
}
c.push(table(['Comparison', '\u0394E median (P10 to P90) %', '\u0394L median (P10 to P90) yr', 'P(adv, mat)'], brows, [4000, 2200, 2160, 1000], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`Against the blended reference, shell concrete at r = 25% has carbon higher by a median ${f0(BLc('A', 'P1_r25m_vs_blend50').dE_pct[1])}% in the pavement case (strength matched) and a shorter life, and the joint-advantage probability is ${f2(BLc('A', 'P1_r25m_vs_blend50').p_adv_mat)} in all three cases at r = 25% and 50%. A realistic low-clinker concrete already delivers the carbon cut that shell replacement offers, with better chloride durability; in the framework, shell powder at the replacement levels studied here is not a competitor to slag blends but at best a complement, and its value must be sought where slag is not available or in the shell\u2019s other uses (Sandler 2026b).`));
c.push(TC('scan', 'Sensitivity to exposure severity and load class for P1 at r = 25% (30 \u00B5m): median life and P(\u22655% carbon cut and non-inferior life), same w/b and strength-matched.'));
const scanRows = [];
const lab = { sev0_load1: 'mild exposure, medium load', sev1_load1: 'moderate exposure, medium load', sev2_load1: 'severe exposure, medium load', sev1_load0: 'moderate exposure, low load', sev1_load2: 'moderate exposure, high load' };
for (const cs of ['A', 'B', 'C']) for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(R.scans[cs])) { const vm = R.scans_matched[cs][k]; scanRows.push([`${cs}: ${lab[k]}`, f1(v.L_opc[1]), f1(v.L[1]), f2(v.p_adv_mat), f1(vm.L[1]), f2(vm.p_adv_mat)]); }
c.push(table(['Case and condition', 'OPC life P50', 'Same w/b: life P50', 'P(adv, mat)', 'Matched: life P50', 'P(adv, mat)'], scanRows, [3000, 1100, 1450, 1250, 1350, 1210], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`Across exposure severity and load class the joint-advantage probability for P1 at r = 25% ranges from ${f2(Math.min(...scanRows.map((r) => Number(r[3]))))} to ${f2(Math.max(...scanRows.map((r) => Number(r[3]))))} for same-w/b mixes and from ${f2(Math.min(...scanRows.map((r) => Number(r[5]))))} to ${f2(Math.max(...scanRows.map((r) => Number(r[5]))))} for strength-matched mixes; no condition gives a high probability, and the highest values occur where the OPC reference is censored at the horizon (dam) or the exposure is mild.`));

// ---------------- 7 Uncertainty and falsification ----------------
c.push(H1('7. Uncertainty and falsification'));
c.push(H2('7.1 Falsification maps'));
c.push(...figure('fals', 'fig6_falsification.png', 'Probability of a joint advantage (carbon cut of at least 5% and non-inferior service life) for P1 as a function of replacement fraction and median particle size, same-w/b (top) and strength-matched (bottom) designs. Grey cells mark strength-matched designs for which matching is infeasible in more than half of the draws.', 6.4));
c.push(P(`The joint-advantage region is small. For the pavement and culvert cases the same-w/b maps reach at most ${f2(Math.max(...R.studies.A.rows.filter((r) => r.path === 'P1' && !r.match && r.r <= 0.5).map((r) => r.p_adv_mat)))} and ${f2(Math.max(...R.studies.C.rows.filter((r) => r.path === 'P1' && !r.match && r.r <= 0.5).map((r) => r.p_adv_mat)))} for r \u2264 50%, and the strength-matched maps reach up to ${f2(Math.max(...R.studies.A.rows.filter((r) => r.path === 'P1' && r.match && r.matched >= 0.5).map((r) => r.p_adv_mat)))} for the pavement and ${f2(Math.max(...R.studies.C.rows.filter((r) => r.path === 'P1' && r.match && r.matched >= 0.5).map((r) => r.p_adv_mat)))} for the culvert, wherever matching is possible in at least half of the draws. The dam map is non-empty at r = 10% mainly for the censoring reason explained in Section 6.2. With the strict definition (any carbon reduction), matched mixes appear to succeed in ${pc(A25m.p_adv)} of pavement draws at r = 25%, but the carbon change is ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1])}% (P10\u2013P90 ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[0])} to ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[2])}%), so the material threshold of 5% is used.`));
c.push(H2('7.2 Pathway P2'));
c.push(...figure('p2', 'fig7_p2.png', 'P2 pathway: median change in embodied carbon against the fraction of limestone replaced by shell in raw meal, for three levels of biogenic credit.', 6.3));
c.push(P(`For P2 the durability effect is negligible (median life change ${sg(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dL[1], 2)} yr in the pavement case from the impurity penalty, none in the dam and culvert cases), so the outcome is determined by carbon accounting. Without credit P2 is falsified: embodied carbon is higher in ${pc(1 - row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).p_E)} of draws. With a 50% or 100% biogenic credit, carbon falls by ${f1(-row2('A', 1.0, 0.5).dE_pct[1])}% and ${f1(-row2('A', 1.0, 1.0).dE_pct[1])}% and the joint advantage is reached in ${pc(row2('C', 1.0, 0.5).p_adv_mat)}\u2013${pc(row2('B', 1.0, 0.5).p_adv_mat)} of dam and culvert draws (for the pavement, the strict non-inferiority test fails in most draws because of the small strength penalty; with a 5% life tolerance the probability is ${pc(row2('A', 1.0, 0.5).p_adv_tol5)}). Whether shell calcination CO\u2082 may be treated as biogenic is a methodological and regulatory question that this model cannot resolve; the credit should be reported as a scenario, not a property.`));
c.push(H2('7.3 Model-form uncertainty and sensitivity'));
c.push(TC('chi', 'Dependence of the median life difference (\u0394L, years) for P1 at r = 25% on the diffusivity model weight \u03C7_D (0 = porosity mapping, 1 = cement-only w/c mapping).'));
const chiRows = X.chi_A_u.map((b, i) => [`${b.bin[0].toFixed(2)}\u2013${b.bin[1].toFixed(2)}`, sg(X.chi_A_u[i].dL50), sg(X.chi_A_m[i].dL50), sg(X.chi_C_u[i].dL50), sg(X.chi_C_m[i].dL50)]);
c.push(table(['\u03C7_D range', 'Pavement: same w/b', 'Pavement: matched', 'Culvert: same w/b', 'Culvert: matched'], chiRows, [1800, 1900, 1900, 1900, 1860], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P(`The sign of the same-w/b effect is robust to the diffusivity mapping (negative in all bins), whereas the median life difference of the matched design ranges from ${sg(X.chi_A_m[0].dL50)} yr to ${sg(X.chi_A_m[3].dL50)} yr in the pavement case as the pessimistic mapping is weighted more heavily. The matched-design result also depends on the assumed filler shift \u03C9 of the ageing exponent, which favours filler-bearing mixes and has no experimental support here.`));
c.push(...figure('sobol', 'fig8_sobol.png', 'Total-order Sobol indices (eight largest, with 95% bootstrap intervals) for the difference in service life (top) and in embodied carbon (bottom) between P1 at r = 25% and OPC, in each case.', 6.4));
c.push(P(`The life difference is controlled by ${top('A', 'dL', 4)} in the pavement case, by ${top('B', 'dL', 4)} in the dam case, and by ${top('C', 'dL', 4)} in the culvert case; the sums of the total-order indices exceed one, indicating interactions. The first-order Jansen estimates were noisy (the sums of first-order indices for the three life-difference analyses were ${f2(R.sobol.A.dL.sum_S1)}, ${f2(R.sobol.B.dL.sum_S1)} and ${f2(R.sobol.C.dL.sum_S1)}, whereas a stable estimate lies between 0 and 1) because of the many parameters and the censored, tie-heavy output, and are not reported. The carbon difference is controlled by logistics and energy inputs: ${top('A', 'dE', 3)}. The shell haul distance alone explains ${pc(R.sobol.A.dE.ST[R.sobol.A.dE.names.indexOf('dist_sh')])} of the carbon variance for the pavement, which means that carbon claims for shell concrete cannot be separated from the supply-chain geography.`));


c.push(H2('7.4 Alternative filler\u2013porosity\u2013transport formulations'));
c.push(P(`The primary formulation applies k_f to strength only. Two alternatives were tested with the same pooled distribution: k_f also refines the capillary porosity (and therefore the chloride diffusivity and the freeze\u2013thaw saturation), and k_f scales with particle fineness. The size-dependent formulation fits worse: with it, no prior draw reproduces the Abbas and Kudukkan calibration mixes within the tolerance (best-fit k_f ${f2(VALS.per_study_kf.ABBAS.best_grid_kf)}, near the edge of the prior) or the 2026 mortar data (best-fit ${f2(VALS.per_study_kf.MEX.best_grid_kf)}, at the bound), against ${f0(VAL.per_study_kf.ABBAS.n_accepted)} accepted draws for the strength-only form, and it is not carried further. The porosity-coupled form leaves strength unchanged but changes the durability conclusions (${T('alt')}): the pavement joint-advantage probability for the matched design at r = 25% rises from ${f2(ALT.claims.strength_only.A.r25m.p_adv_mat)} to ${f2(ALT.claims.porosity_coupled.A.r25m.p_adv_mat)}, the dam value from ${f2(ALT.claims.strength_only.B.r25m.p_adv_mat)} to ${f2(ALT.claims.porosity_coupled.B.r25m.p_adv_mat)} and the culvert value from ${f2(ALT.claims.strength_only.C.r25m.p_adv_mat)} to ${f2(ALT.claims.porosity_coupled.C.r25m.p_adv_mat)}. The durability conclusion is therefore as sensitive to the unmeasured coupling between the filler and the pore structure as it is to the strength calibration, and the primary results are the conservative end of a bracket.`));
c.push(TC('alt', 'Strength-only (primary) and porosity-coupled formulations of k_f: P(\u22655% carbon cut and non-inferior life), median life difference (yr) and median 28-day chloride diffusivity ratio to OPC (r = 25% same w/b).'));
const altRows = [];
for (const [c2, nm] of [['A', 'Pavement'], ['B', 'Dam'], ['C', 'Culvert']]) for (const [k, lab] of [['r25u', '25% same w/b'], ['r25m', '25% matched'], ['r50m', '50% matched']]) {
  const a = ALT.claims.strength_only[c2][k], b = ALT.claims.porosity_coupled[c2][k];
  altRows.push([`${nm}: ${lab}`, `${f2(a.p_adv_mat)} / ${f2(b.p_adv_mat)}`, `${sg(a.dL[1])} / ${sg(b.dL[1])}`, k === 'r25u' ? `${f2(ALT.claims.strength_only[c2].D28_ratio_r25u[1])} / ${f2(ALT.claims.porosity_coupled[c2].D28_ratio_r25u[1])}` : '\u2013']);
}
c.push(table(['Case and design', 'P(adv, mat): strength-only / coupled', '\u0394L P50 (yr)', 'D\u2082\u2088 ratio P50'], altRows, [3000, 2700, 1800, 1860], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(H2('7.5 Ablation: original model, single-study calibration and pooled calibration'));
c.push(P(`The comparison with earlier versions of the model is given in ${T('abl')} and ${F('abl')}. With shell as an inert filler (the original model) the pavement joint-advantage probability at r = 25% is ${f2(row0('A', 0.25, 30, false).p_adv_mat)} (same w/b) and ${f2(row0('A', 0.25, 30, true).p_adv_mat)} (matched), and strength-matched carbon cuts are ${sg(row0('A', 0.25, 30, true).dE_pct[1])}%; calibrated on Abbas and Kudukkan alone they are ${f2(pcl('A', 'r25u').p_adv_mat)}, ${f2(pcl('A', 'r25m').p_adv_mat)} and ${sg(pcl('A', 'r25m').dE_pct[1])}%; with the pooled distribution ${f2(A25.p_adv_mat)}, ${f2(A25m.p_adv_mat)} and ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1])}%. The conclusion that a joint advantage is rare holds in all three, but its size depends on the filler efficiency, which is the least certain strength parameter and is not a property of the material alone.`));
c.push(TC('abl', 'Ablation of the P1 results across model variants (r = 25%, 30 \u00B5m): median carbon change (%), median 28-day strength relative to OPC and P(\u22655% carbon cut and non-inferior life): original (k_f = 0) / Abbas-calibrated / pooled (primary).'));
const ablRows = [];
for (const [c2, nm] of [['A', 'Pavement'], ['B', 'Dam'], ['C', 'Culvert']]) for (const [k, lab, mt] of [['r25u', '25% same w/b', false], ['r25m', '25% matched', true]]) {
  const o = row0(c2, 0.25, 30, mt), a = pcl(c2, k), pl = row1(c2, 0.25, 30, mt);
  ablRows.push([`${nm}: ${lab}`, `${sg(o.dE_pct[1], 0)} / ${sg(a.dE_pct[1], 0)} / ${sg(pl.dE_pct[1], 0)}`, `${f2(o.fc28[1] / o.fc28_opc[1])} / ${f2(a.fc28[1] / a.fc28_opc[1])} / ${f2(pl.fc28[1] / pl.fc28_opc[1])}`, `${f2(o.p_adv_mat)} / ${f2(a.p_adv_mat)} / ${f2(pl.p_adv_mat)}`]);
}
c.push(table(['Case and design', '\u0394E P50 (%)', 'f_c28 vs OPC', 'P(adv, mat)'], ablRows, [3000, 2200, 2100, 2060], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(...figure('abl', 'fig13_ablation.png', 'Probability of a joint advantage (carbon cut of at least 5% and non-inferior life) for P1 at r = 25% (30 \u00B5m) under three model variants: original inert filler, calibration on a single scallop-shell study, and the pooled random-effects distribution used for the primary results.', 6.2));
// ---------------- 8 Discussion ----------------
c.push(H1('8. Discussion'));
c.push(H2('8.1 Physical interpretation'));
c.push(P(`The main results follow from three mechanisms. First, shell powder is a diluent: removing cement lowers strength and raises porosity almost in proportion, so any benefit must come from a filler effect. The size of that effect is the least certain strength parameter: it is ${f2(KFE.KWON)} to ${f2(KFE.ABIN)} across four studies, and it moves the pavement joint-advantage probability from about ${f2(row0('A', 0.25, 30, false).p_adv_mat)} (inert filler) to ${f2(A25.p_adv_mat)}\u2013${f2(A25m.p_adv_mat)} (pooled) at r = 25% (Section 7.5). Second, embodied carbon is dominated by cement, so replacing cement lowers carbon in proportion to r at constant w/b; at constant strength the added binder re-introduces cement, and the median carbon cut falls from ${sg(A25.dE_pct[1], 0)}% to ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1], 0)}% at r = 25%, with an interval that includes net increases. Third, the carbon benefit of P2 is tied to a convention (biogenic credit), since the calcination CO\u2082 per kg of CaCO\u2083 is fixed by stoichiometry. The dam and culvert results show where shell concrete might be advantageous (low replacement in mass concrete) and where it is not (chloride-controlled marine exposure), and the blended-cement comparison (Section 6.4) shows that a conventional low-clinker concrete already delivers a larger carbon cut than shell replacement at the levels modelled.`));
c.push(H2('8.2 Assessment of the white paper\u2019s claims'));
c.push(TC('assess', 'Source claims restated as hypotheses with model outputs and the validation each would need.'));
c.push(table(['Claim', 'Model output (this study)', 'Status', 'Validation needed'], [
  ['Up to 80% CO\u2082 reduction', `${sg(claims.r100.dE_p50)}% only at r = 100% (no strength); ${sg(A25.dE_pct[1])}% / ${sg(A50.dE_pct[1])}% at r = 25 / 50% (same w/b); ${sg(claims.r25.dE_matched_p50)}% / ${sg(claims.r50.dE_matched_p50)}% matched; P2 ${sg(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct[1])}% without credit`, 'Not supported', 'Plant-specific cradle-to-gate LCA with defined boundary and functional unit'],
  ['Thermal resistance to 2,400 \u00B0F', 'Not modelled', 'Not supported (see text)', 'Furnace tests with residual strength; thermogravimetry of the shell binder'],
  ['100% limestone substitution', `P2, \u03C1 = 100%: carbon ${sg(row2('A', 1.0, 0.0).dE_pct[1])}% (no credit); durability unchanged`, 'Feasible chemically; no carbon benefit without credit', 'Raw-meal burnability, clinker chemistry, chloride and alkali limits'],
  ['Accelerated hydration', `\u03B1(1 d): ${f2(alpha.opc[0])} (OPC) vs ${f2(alpha.r25[0])} (r = 25%); strength ${pc(claims.r25.fc_rel)} of OPC`, 'Marginal acceleration; strength lower', 'Isothermal calorimetry; setting time; strength at 1\u2013365 d'],
  ['Reduced shrinkage', `Cracking probability ${pc(A25.p_crack_sh_opc)} \u2192 ${pc(A25.p_crack_sh)} at r = 25% (same w/b); ${pc(A25m.p_crack_sh)} when matched`, 'Supported only as dilution', 'Restrained-ring and free-shrinkage tests'],
  ['Superior acid resistance', `50-yr front ${sg(100 * (X.front_C.r25u[1] / X.front_C.opc[1] - 1), 0)}% (same w/b) vs ${sg(100 * (X.front_C.r25m[1] / X.front_C.opc[1] - 1), 0)}% (matched); particle-size dependent`, 'Conditional', 'Acid immersion with mass loss and depth of degradation'],
  ['Higher structural durability', `\u0394L P50 ${sg(A25.dL[1])} yr (pavement) and ${sg(C25.dL[1])} yr (culvert) at r = 25% same w/b; ${sg(A25m.dL[1])} / ${sg(C25m.dL[1])} yr matched`, 'Not supported at same w/b; conditional if matched', 'Chloride migration, freeze\u2013thaw, fatigue and abrasion tests on matched mixes'],
], [1700, 3300, 1700, 2660], { boldFirst: true }));
c.push(gap());
c.push(P('**On the thermal claim.** 2,400 \u00B0F is about 1,316 \u00B0C. Calcite decomposes to CaO and CO\u2082 at roughly 900 \u00B0C (about 1,650 \u00B0F) at atmospheric CO\u2082 pressure (standard thermodynamic data; Chase 1998), so a calcium-carbonate-rich binder is not expected to withstand 2,400 \u00B0F; even conventional concrete suffers substantial strength loss and spalling at much lower temperatures. No model of fire behaviour is attempted here and the claim should be removed unless supported by formulation-specific tests.'));
c.push(H2('8.3 Limitations'));
['No new experiments are reported. Shell-specific parameters (\u03BD, \u03B2_p, \u03BA_o, \u03C9, k_imp) are literature-style ranges; only the strength model was calibrated, on one published scallop-shell dataset (three mixes, replacement 14\u201340% of the binder) whose strengths are partly read from text and derived from moduli, with the held-out mix between the calibration mixes. Independent testing used 11 oyster-shell points from three studies, one of them a secondary transcription and one with coarse particle fractions and an unclear replacement definition; k_f differs in sign and size between studies, so the pooled distribution describes heterogeneity and not a material property, and it ignores correlations with the other parameters.',
 'Chloride, acid and freeze\u2013thaw transport remain uncalibrated: paste porosity counts cement only, the filler efficiency is not applied to transport, and the claims that depend on life (Section 7.4) are therefore bracketed rather than determined.',
 'The OPC reference lives are anchored to plausible design lives by adjusting OPC-only parameters; results are relative, and the absolute lives are not predictions.',
 'The diffusivity mapping for filler-bearing pastes is an extrapolation of a relation calibrated for OPC; the two mappings bracket but do not validate it.',
 'The reference is plain OPC concrete; dam and marine concretes normally contain supplementary cementitious materials, which would change both carbon and durability baselines.',
 'Mechanisms are combined additively and one-dimensionally; carbonation, alkali\u2013silica reaction, sulfate attack, reinforcement corrosion kinetics, calcite dissolution mass loss and the interaction of cracking with all transport processes are not modelled; surface chloride is constant.',
 'Carbon factors are generic screening values; the avoided-landfill credit, shell logistics and biogenic accounting are uncertain and dominate the carbon variance.',
 'The strength-matching rule (adding binder at constant water) is only one of several ways to achieve equal strength and is not applicable where the model strength saturates with binder.'].forEach((t) => c.push(B(t)));
c.push(H2('8.4 Novelty and validation requirements'));
c.push(P('The contribution is not the finding that fine calcium carbonate acts as a filler, which is established for limestone, but a framework that couples processing-dependent carbon with hydration, transport and damage, evaluates each shell mix against its own OPC reference under shared uncertainty, and reports where the joint advantage fails. To be a validated model, the following would be needed: (i) isothermal calorimetry, porosimetry and strength development on shell-powder pastes and concretes at several replacement levels, particle sizes and organic contents; (ii) chloride migration/diffusion, acid-immersion, freeze\u2013thaw, fatigue and abrasion tests on same-w/b and strength-matched mixes; (iii) further out-of-sample comparisons of predicted and measured properties on oyster shell and on durability indicators, beyond the four strength datasets used in Section 4.4; and (iv) a plant- and supply-chain-specific life-cycle inventory.'));

// ---------------- 9 Conclusions ----------------
c.push(H1('9. Conclusions'));
c.push(B(`A coupled hydration\u2013transport\u2013damage\u2013carbon framework with paired shell/OPC comparison, Latin hypercube Monte Carlo, Sobol sensitivity and a falsification region was developed and verified numerically. Its inert-filler strength model was rejected by published scallop-shell data (predicted ${f2(KF0.pred.S30F15[1])} against ${f2(CAL.obs.S30F15)} relative strength at 40% replacement).`));
c.push(B(`An effective strength-efficiency coefficient calibrated on one scallop-shell dataset does not transfer: on 11 independent oyster-shell points it has a mean absolute error of ${f2(CMP[mname.abbas].all.MAE)}, no better than dilution alone (${f2(CMP[mname.bol].all.MAE)}), and the coefficient differs between studies from ${f2(Math.min(...Object.values(KFE)))} to ${f2(Math.max(...Object.values(KFE)))}. The primary results therefore use a pooled distribution that expresses this heterogeneity.`));
c.push(B(`At r = 25% and constant w/b, shell replacement lowers embodied carbon by ${f0(-A25.dE_pct[1])}% and strength to ${pc(claims.r25.fc_rel)} of OPC and shortens median life by ${f1(-A25.dL[1])} yr (pavement) and ${f1(-C25.dL[1])} yr (culvert); with strength matched the median carbon cut is ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[1], 0)}% (P10\u2013P90 ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[0], 0)} to ${sg(A25m.dE_pct[2], 0)}%) and the life change ${sg(A25m.dL[1])} yr. The 80% carbon claim is reached only when strength is lost.`));
c.push(B(`The probability of a carbon cut of at least 5% with non-inferior life is at most ${f2(Math.max(A25m.p_adv_mat, A25.p_adv_mat))} (pavement), ${f2(Math.max(B25.p_adv_mat, row1('B', 0.25, 30, true).p_adv_mat))} (dam, largely censoring) and ${f2(Math.max(C25.p_adv_mat, C25m.p_adv_mat))} (marine culvert) at r = 25%; it is ${f2(0)} against a 50% slag blended cement, which cuts carbon by about ${f0(-BLc('A', 'blend50').dE_pct_vs_opc[1])}% on its own.`));
c.push(B(`These durability conclusions are conditional on an unmeasured coupling between the filler and the pore structure: if k_f also refines the pores, the pavement probability at r = 25% (matched) rises from ${f2(ALT.claims.strength_only.A.r25m.p_adv_mat)} to ${f2(ALT.claims.porosity_coupled.A.r25m.p_adv_mat)}. Chloride, acid and freeze\u2013thaw transport remain uncalibrated.`));
// (removed: superseded by the pooled results)
c.push(B('Shell calcium carbonate used as limestone feedstock (P2) does not reduce carbon unless calcination CO\u2082 is credited as biogenic; durability is essentially unchanged.'));
c.push(B('Carbon claims for shell concrete are controlled by supply-chain logistics, not by material chemistry, and durability claims by the filler efficiency and the diffusivity of filler-bearing pastes; these are the quantities to measure first.'));
c.push(B('Shell used in concrete is not available as reef substrate; the allocation of recovered shell between the two uses is treated in a companion paper (Sandler 2026b).'));

